The tiling must have exactly two vertices $\nu$ and $\sigma$ (as the \emph{north} and \emph{south} poles) of degree $>3$, and the degrees at the two vertices must be the same. The first picture in Figure \ref{map5} is the view of the tiling from the north pole $\nu$. All the outward rays converge at the south pole $\sigma$. The second picture is our usual way of drawing maps, from the viewpoint of the equator.

An immediate consequence is that, if a combinatorial pentagonal tiling is not the earth map tiling in Figure \ref{map5}, then every vertex of degree $>3$ must have another vertex of degree $>3$ within distance $4$. 


\begin{proof}
Starting with a vertex $\nu$ of degree $k>3$, we construct the pentagonal tiles layer by layer. We label the edges emanating from $\nu$ by $k$ distinct indices $i$ and denote the other end of the $i$-th edge by $x_i$. Since $x_*$ are of distance $1$ from $\nu$, they have degree $3$. If two $x_*$ are identified, say $x_i=x_j$, then we get a $2$-gon with $\nu$ and $x_i$ as the two vertices. Since $x_i$ has degree $3$, there are two possibilities for the $2$-gon, given in Figure \ref{xx}. The first appears in Figure \ref{impossible}, and the outside of the second is the first. This proves that all $x_*$ are distinct.


\begin{figure}[htp]
\centering
