We put any meridian in the vertical position and develop the tiling on the right side (or the east side).  We find that the development comes back to the original meridian we started with. Therefore the same development can repeat itself. We call the tiling between adjacent meridians \emph{timezones}. By glueing several timezones bounded by the same meridians, we get the earth map tilings.

Figure \ref{map1} gives the earth map tiling with the meridian $[1]$ (indicated by the thick line), in both the polar and equator views. Figure \ref{map2} gives the earth map tiling with the meridian $[2]$, which also includes the ``meridian'' $[3']$ (so $[3']$ cannot be a meridian, see proof below). Figure \ref{map3} gives the earth map tiling with the meridian $[3]$.

