2858:For example, we start with the meridian $[3]$ and develop the tiles in Figure \ref{zone}. In the first picture, the shaded tile is newly created, together with a new vertex indicated by the dot. If the dot is identified with the south pole $\sigma$, then we get the second picture, and we can apply Lemma \ref{cycle} to the ``obvious'' simple closed path of length $6$.
2958:If the dot is identified with $x$, then we get the third picture. However, Lemma \ref{cycle} can be applied neither to the inside nor to the outside of the thick simple closed path, because either side contains a vertex of degree $>3$ in the interior. To resolve the problem, we may either change the simple closed path to the one in the fourth picture and apply Lemma \ref{cycle} to the outside, or we may modify the simple closed path in the third picture to the one in the fifth picture and apply Lemma \ref{cycle}.
2995:Now we deal with the third thing. We start with any meridian and add successive timezones. As long as we have at least two edges available at each pole, we may continue adding timezone. If there are exactly two edges available at each poles, then we can complete the final timezone and get the earth map tiling. This fails exactly when we have no or one edge left in one pole and at least one edge left at the other pole. Figure \ref{special2} describes the failing cases for the meridian $[3]$. Applying Lemma \ref{cycle}, these failing cases cannot happen. The same reason applies to the other meridians.
3002:\subsection*{Acknowledgements}
3024:\newblock Data structures and algorithms for tilings, I. Tilings of the plane.
3049:\newblock {\em Angles in spherical pentagonal tilings}.

codex
The paper’s native object is indeed an embedded spherical graph whose face boundaries are simple 5-cycles and whose vertex degrees are at least three. The key early warning is also explicit: the paper cites algorithmic enumeration via Delaney symbols, while its own main positive objects—the five earth-map families—are constructed and classified by short, repeated “timezone” developments. I’m checking whether that leaves a genuine Track B compression gap or merely makes the requested witness a direct read-off.
